---
/**
 * Privacy — plain-language privacy note, served on theourgia.com.
 *
 * The CookieNotice (mounted site-wide via BaseLayout) links here. It
 * previously pointed at docs.theourgia.com, which has no DNS; this page
 * mirrors the canonical wording from
 * ``docs/site/src/content/docs/start/privacy.md`` so the link resolves
 * on the site itself.
 *
 * Honesty law: state absences plainly, never advertise a capability the
 * instance has gated off. Federation transport is DISABLED on
 * theourgia.com today, so the outbound-calls list marks it as not yet
 * enabled rather than implying it is live.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const MAXW = "820px";

const NOT_COLLECTED = [
  "Usage metrics sent to any third party",
  "Third-party analytics scripts (Google Analytics, Plausible, Fathom, PostHog, and the like)",
  "Tracking of which pages you visit, which features you use, or how long you stay",
  "“Anonymous” usage IDs reported anywhere",
  "Opt-in telemetry — it is not offered at all",
];

// What can leave the device / server, and when. Each entry is
// user-initiated; nothing here fires on its own. Federation transport is
// currently gated off on theourgia.com, so it is marked as such.
const OUTBOUND = [
  {
    label: "Email delivery",
    when: "when you send a newsletter or a notification, or use the contact form (delivered via Resend).",
    live: true,
  },
  {
    label: "Stripe",
    when: "only when a purchase is made, between your Stripe session and Stripe.",
    live: true,
  },
  {
    label: "Backup target (e.g. Cloudflare R2)",
    when: "when a scheduled backup you configured runs.",
    live: true,
  },
  {
    label: "Cloudflare DNS API",
    when: "for TLS certificate renewal (DNS-01 challenge).",
    live: true,
  },
  {
    label: "Federation & ActivityPub messages",
    when: "the protocol ships in the software, but federation transport on theourgia.com is not yet enabled — no instance-to-instance or Fediverse traffic leaves this host today.",
    live: false,
  },
  {
    label: "Anthropic API",
    when: "if and only if you have opted into the AI agent integration (not enabled by default).",
    live: false,
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Privacy · Theourgia">
  <main
    id="om-main"
    style={`max-width:${MAXW};margin:0 auto;padding:64px clamp(16px,5vw,28px) 80px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:48px`}
  >
    <a
      href="/"
      style="font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:12px;color:var(--ink-mute);text-decoration:none;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase"
      >← Theourgia</a
    >

    <header style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:14px">
      <span
        style="font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:11.5px;color:var(--ink-mute);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.16em"
      >
        What we do and do not collect
      </span>
      <h1
        style="margin:0;font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:44px;letter-spacing:-0.01em;color:var(--ink);line-height:1.15"
      >
        Privacy
      </h1>
      <p
        style="margin:0;font-family:var(--font-serif);font-size:19px;line-height:1.55;color:var(--ink-soft);max-width:640px"
      >
        Theourgia ships zero telemetry. It does not phone home. This note
        states, in plain language, what stays on your device or server, what
        can leave it, and exactly when.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:14px">
      <h2
        style="margin:0;font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:24px;color:var(--ink);letter-spacing:.01em"
      >
        Your record lives where you keep it
      </h2>
      <p style="margin:0;font-family:var(--font-serif);font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--ink-soft)">
        The mobile app keeps your journal, entities, divinations, and workings
        on your device first. When you use a hosted instance, that record lives
        on the server you signed into — theourgia.com if that is the instance
        you chose, or your own machine if you self-host. Either way you can
        export everything and leave at any time.
      </p>
      <p style="margin:0;font-family:var(--font-serif);font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--ink-soft)">
        Two encryption modes are offered per item: <strong>server-side at
        rest</strong> (the server holds the keys; supports full-text search) and
        <strong>zero-knowledge client-side</strong> (encrypted in your browser;
        the server holds only ciphertext and even the operator cannot read it —
        used for initiation records, oath-ledger entries, and anything you mark
        sealed).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px">
      <h2
        style="margin:0;font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:24px;color:var(--ink);letter-spacing:.01em"
      >
        What Theourgia does not do
      </h2>
      <p style="margin:0;font-family:var(--font-serif);font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--ink-soft)">
        This is a hard guarantee, checked by an automated test in CI: a change
        that introduced an unauthorised outbound network call would fail the
        build. Theourgia does not:
      </p>
      <ul style="margin:0;padding-left:22px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px;font-family:var(--font-serif);font-size:15px;line-height:1.55;color:var(--ink-soft)">
        {NOT_COLLECTED.map((item) => (
          <li>{item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px">
      <h2
        style="margin:0;font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:24px;color:var(--ink);letter-spacing:.01em"
      >
        What can leave the device or server, and when
      </h2>
      <p style="margin:0;font-family:var(--font-serif);font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--ink-soft)">
        Every outbound call below is explicitly user-initiated; none fires on
        its own. Where a destination is not yet enabled on theourgia.com, that
        is stated.
      </p>
      <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px">
        {OUTBOUND.map((o) => (
          <div
            style="background-color:var(--bg-2);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:12px;padding:16px 20px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px"
          >
            <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap">
              <span style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:16px;color:var(--ink)">{o.label}</span>
              {!o.live && (
                <span style="font-family:var(--font-ui);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--ink-mute);border:1px solid var(--line-2);border-radius:999px;padding:2px 9px">Not yet enabled here</span>
              )}
            </div>
            <p style="margin:0;font-family:var(--font-serif);font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.55;color:var(--ink-soft)">{o.when}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p style="margin:0;font-family:var(--font-serif);font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--ink-soft)">
        If a new outbound destination is ever added, it is documented and gated
        behind explicit user action.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:14px">
      <h2
        style="margin:0;font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:24px;color:var(--ink);letter-spacing:.01em"
      >
        Cookies
      </h2>
      <p style="margin:0;font-family:var(--font-serif);font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--ink-soft)">
        The only cookie the platform sets is a single first-party session
        cookie (<code>theourgia_session</code>) — HttpOnly, Secure, strictly
        essential, and set only once you sign in. There are no analytics,
        advertising, or third-party cookies, so there is nothing to accept or
        reject. The site-wide cookie notice remembers itself in your browser's
        local storage — no cookie of its own.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:14px">
      <h2
        style="margin:0;font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:24px;color:var(--ink);letter-spacing:.01em"
      >
        GDPR
      </h2>
      <ul style="margin:0;padding-left:22px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px;font-family:var(--font-serif);font-size:15px;line-height:1.55;color:var(--ink-soft)">
        <li><strong>Right to access</strong> — one-click export of all your data.</li>
        <li><strong>Right to erasure</strong> — full deletion, with documented limits for federated content that has already propagated to other parties.</li>
        <li><strong>Data portability</strong> — exports in standard JSON / MBF format.</li>
        <li><strong>Privacy by design</strong> — minimal collection, encryption at rest, no third-party trackers.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);padding-top:28px">
      <p style="margin:0;font-family:var(--font-serif);font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--ink-soft)">
        The full trust model is documented in{" "}
        <a
          href="https://github.com/SAntonopoulou/theourgia/blob/main/ARCHITECTURE.md"
          rel="noopener"
          style="color:var(--accent)"
          >ARCHITECTURE.md §5</a
        >. Questions: <a href="mailto:contact@theourgia.com" style="color:var(--accent)">contact@theourgia.com</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
